## Product architecture: Reality Trace

The dashboard will add a cross-cutting **Reality Trace** that binds every view to a human question and a decision boundary.

| Trace stage | Human question | Persisted record | Allowed result |
|---|---|---|---|
| Situation | “What happened and what needs deciding?” | Scenario, decision context, owner, impact | Concrete question; not a conclusion. |
| Claim | “What exact relation must hold?” | Claim class, predicate, assumptions, non-claims | Bounded check specification. |
| Evidence | “Which data and transformations support it?” | Dataset IDs/hashes, provenance, access, uncertainty | Completeness or explicit missing evidence. |
| Evaluation | “What was actually evaluated?” | Structural or external analysis receipt | Scoped result with method/input binding. |
| Review | “Who verified the scope and evidence?” | Gate decisions, signed immutable review events | Admit, block, quarantine, approve, reject, or revision request. |
| Decision support | “What can I safely do or say next?” | Plain-language finding, limitation, next evidence action | Constrained support; never automated real-world judgment. |

The first scenario gallery will make four practical pathways concrete: **data handoff integrity**, **simulation/model constraint checks**, **measurement-study evidence packaging**, and **cross-team claim review**. Each will make clear where mathematics enters, what software actually executes, what evidence is still absent, and what a person can responsibly decide next.

## Workstream A — Signed release-artifact verification

Create a local/offline signed-release contract around `RELEASE_MANIFEST.json`:

| Planned artifact | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `RELEASE_ATTESTATION.dsse.json` | DSSE envelope over canonical release ID, manifest SHA-256, release boundary, source revision, matrix result, and file-list digest. |
| `policies/release_key_policy.v0.1.json` | Active/revoked public keys, permitted payload type, policy version, and scope; never a private key. |
| `release_verification_report.json` | Terminal status, policy/signer IDs, checked hashes, reason code, and scope limitation. |
| `sov_kernel.py verify-release` | Offline verifier with deterministic JSON output and exit codes. |
| Signed-release guide | One-command verification, key rollover/revocation, failure recovery, and non-claims. |

The verifier will return `verified` only when bundle file digests, manifest binding, DSSE payload, signature, and public-key policy all match; `fail` for a malformed or tampered input; and `unverifiable` for unsigned historical releases, unavailable material, unknown policy versions, or unsupported schemes. The existing unsigned v0.1 bundle will remain **unverifiable**, not be retroactively portrayed as verified.

Implementation steps are: define canonical statement/policy/reason codes; extend existing Ed25519 DSSE functions with test-only signing fixtures; implement the CLI and report; test altered file/manifest/signature, unknown/revoked key, payload mismatch, malformed data, unsigned bundle, and policy rollover; then add this evidence to the release matrix and dashboard feed. No remote KMS, GitHub signing token, cloud key, public transparency service, or production private key is introduced.

## Workstream B — External adapter review workflow

Turn the current `candidate`/`quarantine` validator into a review system that cannot auto-admit code. Persist `adapter_submission`, immutable `submission_revision`, `gate_evidence`, `review_decision`, `review_comment`, `admission_report`, and `release_decision` records. Valid states are `draft → submitted → schema_validating → evidence_pending | reviewing → blocked | quarantined | admitted | rejected`, with `revision_requested` as a branch; every transition records actor, reason, timestamp, old/new state, and artifact hash.

| Role | May do | May not do |
|---|---|---|
| Submitter | Submit manifest, fixture/evidence references, and revisions; answer comments. | Self-admit or replace reviewed evidence. |
| Semantic reviewer | Assess scope, semantics, non-claims, and neutrality. | Alone approve implementation/release. |
| Implementation reviewer | Assess evaluator, fixtures, replay, tamper, and reproducibility evidence. | Convert theory language into a predicate. |
| Release steward | Confirm all eight gates, hashes, version, rollback point, and publish the release decision. | Alter a submitted revision without a new immutable revision. |

The UI will provide a candidate queue; step-by-step submission wizard; eight-gate matrix; evidence/hash cards; reviewer assignment; immutable event timeline; revision diff; and public-safe summary of predicate, scope, version, decision, and non-claims. Gate 4 will visibly compare provenance labels. Gate 7 will link the signed release verification report. Runtime registry admission remains a separate reviewed source-code change.

## Workstream C — Approved external analysis-receipt ingestion and explorer

Accept an external analysis receipt only as a **versioned external record** that is structurally valid, packet-linked, complete in method/provenance/uncertainty fields, and approved through review. Never run an external scientific method silently, invent values, infer links, or call approval “truth.” Lifecycle: `draft → submitted → schema_valid → lineage_valid → pending_review → approved | rejected | quarantined | superseded`. The default explorer displays only approved records.

| Explorer component | User value | Mandatory caveat |
|---|---|---|
| Filter rail/table | Filter by claim class, method/version, dataset hash, access/license, uncertainty, evidence date, and review status. | Filters organize declared metadata, not validity. |
| Coverage dashboard | Shows inventory by method, review state, access, and uncertainty declaration. | Counts are not support strength. |
| Detail / Reality Trace | Displays claim → packet → data hashes → method → uncertainty → reviewers → limitations. | Lineage is not causal/scientific proof. |
| Provenance graph | Traverses explicit packet, dataset, activity, agent, receipt, adapter, and release links. | Missing edges remain visibly missing. |
| Comparison workspace | Compares methods, data identity, assumptions, uncertainty, and disposition. | Similar records are not automatically comparable. |
| Export/replay panel | Downloads canonical records, hashes, verifier reports, and a plain-language summary. | Metadata replay is not re-running external science. |

Start with metadata, hashes, locators, licenses, and access conditions. Do not ingest raw/restricted source data unless a separate storage, consent, access-control, and retention policy is approved.

