## Workstream E — Full legacy-versus-universal kernel report

Produce `LEGACY_TO_UNIVERSAL_KERNEL_COMPARISON_v0.1.md` from repository evidence and release artifacts. Inventory relevant legacy modules/docs, classify each statement as **implemented, partial, planned, archival, or unverified**, then compare it to the isolated current package. The report will include a bridge map: legacy mathematical objects that could become neutral adapters only after a precise finite predicate/fixtures/reference evaluator/non-claim; archival-only material; and items that require empirical rather than structural evidence.

| Axis | Legacy geometry-native kernel | Current universal kernel | Required treatment |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ontology | E8/Hopf/Riemann-Bloch geometry drives semantics. | Theory labels are provenance only; finite predicates are generic. | Explain neutrality; do not call legacy mathematics disproven. |
| Primary job | Intended AI runtime, semantic engine, P2P/consensus, and model-serving environment. | Offline deterministic claim/receipt verifier. | State clearly that the new kernel is not a runtime replacement. |
| Inputs | Corpora, geometry states, inference/node context. | Typed finite objects plus empirical packets. | Map only concrete overlaps; label missing bridges. |
| Evaluation | Runtime/geometric logic mixed with servers/fallback paths. | Code-owned predicates and fail-closed outcomes. | Identify reproducibility gaps without overstating test coverage. |
| Evidence | Broad architecture and geometric claims. | Fixtures, replay, hashes, audit, DSSE fixtures, matrix. | Separate tested contracts from aspirations. |
| Extensibility | Theory-specific modules. | Eight-gate neutral adapters. | Compare admission standards and their limits. |
| Operations | REST/Ollama/MCP/P2P/consensus ambitions. | Explicitly offline; no distributed consensus or production custody. | Publish deferred capabilities rather than imply completion. |

The comparison will answer practical questions: “what can I trust before I share a result?”, “what changed in my model/data?”, “what must be reviewed before this supports a decision?”, and “which legacy ideas are ready for a bounded test rather than a broad interpretation?”

## Workstream F — Research, content gaps, and reusable building blocks

1. Evaluate open-source patterns—not mandatory dependencies—for future compatibility: Sigstore/Cosign/SLSA for signed-artifact ecosystems; AiiDA Explorer/Data Provenance Explorer patterns for trace/filter UX. Review license, maintenance, offline fit, and threat model before adoption.
2. Conduct a task-based information-architecture audit for release verification, adapter submission, gate review, evidence discovery, uncertainty interpretation, and trace-back to data.
3. Produce a content-gap report centered on user outcomes: what the kernel is for, why a receipt changes work, how to review evidence, why uncertainty/provenance matter, and what no status can prove. Use public SERP sampling unless a target domain and SEO exports are supplied.
4. Defer a quantitative 300-keyword workbook until the user provides target country/worldwide scope and a data source (connected SEO tool, export, or public-data fallback). Do not invent volume, difficulty, CPC, traffic, or competitor metrics.
5. Assess Manus API v2 only as an optional research-task integration using structured output, explicit authorization, stored task IDs, webhook verification, and review-before-ingestion. It remains outside signing, release verification, and final approval.

