## Prior completed ecosystem build plan (archival context)

## Goal

Turn the completed **offline, theory-agnostic universal kernel** into a coherent ecosystem that people can understand, use, extend, verify, and govern. The outcome is not a larger opaque “engine.” It is a documented and reproducible platform with a small kernel at its center, supported by explicit claim workflows, adapter packages, empirical-evidence tooling, scripts, release artifacts, user guidance, and dashboards.

The active kernel already has a bounded public receipt model, six finite structural adapters, replayable provenance, local audit evidence, offline Ed25519 fixtures, durable quorum/audit components, a command-line workflow, a shared six-adapter integration fixture pack, and a reproducible release matrix. The current evidence baseline is **41 focused Python tests and 4 Rust tests**, according to `EMPIRICAL_PACKET_INTEGRATION_MATRIX.log`. This plan treats that as an observed offline baseline, not a production-service claim.

## Guiding decisions

| Decision | Plan position | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| Kernel identity | Preserve the kernel as theory-agnostic and structural. | No framework label may change evaluator behavior or confer credibility. |
| Public experience | Keep the front door simple: claim → declared input/evidence → named check → readable receipt → next action. | Users should not need to understand DSSE, Merkle trees, or tensor notation to learn the outcome. |
| Advanced assurance | Expose replay, hashes, audit attachments, signatures, quorum evidence, and Merkle material as optional inspectable layers. | Evidence becomes available without misleading users about operational guarantees. |
| Empirical work | Maintain empirical claims as a companion layer with external-method bindings. | Structural validation must never be presented as scientific validation. |
| Third-party work | Admit external adapters through the published eight-gate process. | Adapter admission is a governance and reproducibility test, not theory endorsement. |
| Documentation | Publish one canonical documentation hierarchy with generated indexes and archival labels. | Existing documents are numerous; their roles and currentness need to be discoverable. |
| Operational scope | Keep network consensus, production key management, public transparency, and empirical inference outside the current offline release. | This prevents overclaiming and keeps the trusted core small. |

## Target ecosystem architecture

```text
People and research contributors
        │
        ├─ Plain-language guides, tutorials, dashboards, example journeys
        ├─ Claim templates and empirical packet builder
        └─ Third-party adapter starter kit and scorecard
        │
Documentation and developer platform
        │
        ├─ Canonical docs site + API reference + release notes
        ├─ CLI, examples, validators, fixture and release scripts
        └─ Registry of admitted adapters, schemas, fixtures, receipts, and limitations
        │
Offline universal kernel
        │
        ├─ Core receipt / canonicalization / replay / public outcomes
        ├─ Structural adapter registry and universal claim packet contract
        ├─ Empirical packet shape/provenance/uncertainty validator
        └─ Optional local assurance: audit, Merkle, DSSE fixtures, quorum evidence
        │
Explicitly separate follow-on programs
        ├─ External statistical or scientific analysis execution
        ├─ Live key lifecycle and remote trust distribution
        ├─ Public or replicated transparency services
        ├─ Distributed network quorum service
        └─ Formal theorem bridges and empirical validation campaigns
```

## Workstream portfolio

### Wave E0 — Normalize the ecosystem surface

Create a single artifact catalog and ownership map. Classify every existing project document as one of: **canonical specification**, **current implementation guide**, **release evidence**, **tutorial/example**, **archival research**, **superseded**, or **generated output**. The catalog will provide title, version, status, audience, owner role, source path, dependencies, and replacement link where relevant.

Create a canonical `docs/` hierarchy in the repository with a machine-readable documentation manifest. Initial sections should be `start-here`, `concepts`, `how-to`, `reference`, `assurance`, `adapters`, `empirical-evidence`, `governance`, `research-archive`, and `release-evidence`. Historical Geometric Unity material remains accessible only below `research-archive`, with the existing theory-neutral archival notice retained at every entry point.

**Acceptance gate:** no active top-level guide presents a theory-specific ontology as kernel semantics; every canonical document has a status, audience, and source of truth; all archive material has an archival-context banner.

### Wave E1 — Public claim and receipt experience

Standardize the public interface across the CLI, dashboard, documentation, and examples. Build a claim journey library with at least one worked example for each of the six current structural adapters: matrix symmetry, identity, inverse, finite partial order, graph connectivity, and tensor last-index symmetry. Each journey will include valid input, deliberate mutation, plain-language outcome explanation, receipt view, replay step, and stated non-claim.

Package the existing `sov_kernel.py` behavior into a documented command reference with stable command examples. Add a sample-gallery generator that takes fixture packets and renders Markdown/JSON receipt examples, so tutorial receipts are never manually rewritten. Update the dashboard’s Invariant Lab to load these generated examples and their receipt metadata rather than curated constants.

**Acceptance gate:** every public adapter has one generated valid example, one fail example, one unverifiable example, and a synchronized CLI/dashboard/documentation journey.

### Wave E2 — Developer and adapter kit

Create an adapter starter kit under a dedicated template directory. The kit will include a namespaced schema, pure evaluator skeleton, fixture pack, mutation harness, fixture-manifest generator, neutrality test, replay test, non-claims template, review templates, and release manifest script. It will be aligned with `THIRD_PARTY_FRAMEWORK_ADAPTER_SPEC_v0.1.md` and the eight-gate scorecard.

Add a code-owned adapter registry with metadata fields for adapter ID, version, object schema, predicate ID, claimed domain, status, release manifest, test report, and non-claims. The registry must reject unadmitted adapters by default. It must not import callbacks from untrusted packet data or use framework provenance as a dispatch key.

Build a `validate-adapter-package` script that runs the eight gates in an isolated local workspace and emits a machine-readable admission report. Add a “candidate” state that records evidence without making the adapter generally available.

**Acceptance gate:** a sample neutral adapter can be generated from the starter kit, run through all eight gates, and produce a manifest, scorecard, and reproducible report. A deliberately framework-conditioned implementation must fail the neutrality gate.

### Wave E3 — Empirical evidence companion

Promote the empirical packet parser into a documented package boundary with JSON Schema, canonical test fixtures, CLI support, sample packet generator, and an evidence-status report. Preserve the current rule: the parser verifies packet structure, provenance fields, uncertainty description, and analysis binding; it returns `unverifiable` for the empirical statement until an external analysis receipt exists.

Add a versioned **analysis receipt** specification. It will identify the external method/protocol, software or environment identity, exact input dataset IDs/hashes, declared parameters, output schema, assumptions, execution result, limitations, and review state. The receipt must distinguish method execution from scientific interpretation. It must reject absent or inconsistent links back to the empirical packet.

Add an **empirical packet builder** to the dashboard and CLI. The builder will be form-driven and progressive: target quantity → data identity/access/license → provenance activity → uncertainty → method binding → non-claims. It must surface missing evidence as “not ready for analysis” rather than guessing defaults. The first external analysis connector should be a static, documented protocol placeholder—not a live inference service—until a validated method is selected.

**Acceptance gate:** valid, incomplete, inconsistent-lineage, missing-uncertainty-basis, and mismatched-analysis-receipt fixtures have deterministic outcomes. Generated packet examples and the builder produce schema-identical outputs.

### Wave E4 — Assurance and release operations

Consolidate local assurance into a documented evidence bundle format that references receipt provenance, replay output, optional audit event/checkpoint/inclusion proof, optional offline Ed25519 fixture result, and optional durable quorum record. The bundle must label every absent layer as absent and every local-only component as local-only.

Split release automation into deterministic jobs: formatting/static validation, Python tests, Rust tests, shared fixture-manifest verification, adapter package validation, empirical packet validation, release-bundle build, clean-install smoke test, checksum generation, and documentation link/manifest validation. Preserve a fail-closed policy: an unknown schema, missing fixture manifest, invalid documentation manifest, unsupported adapter, or failed replay blocks a release artifact.

Create a release dashboard data feed generated from these artifacts. It should display the latest verified offline release evidence, test counts, fixture hashes, adapter statuses, and explicitly blocked operational work. It must not use static “production-ready” language.

**Acceptance gate:** a clean environment can run one release command and reproduce all generated evidence; a one-character fixture, receipt, packet, adapter, or documentation-manifest mutation fails the appropriate gate.

### Wave E5 — Documentation, learning, and communication system

Create five documentation levels:

| Audience | Deliverables |
|---|---|
| General reader | What the kernel does, the three outcomes, claim process, common misconceptions, and examples. |
| Research contributor | Claim packet guide, empirical evidence workflow, non-claims policy, archive rules, and research register. |
| Adapter author | Starter kit, eight-gate scorecard, schema/predicate conventions, fixture/oracle guide, and release instructions. |
| Advanced auditor | Canonicalization, replay, audit attachments, DSSE fixture status, quorum/Merkle boundaries, and manifest verification. |
| Maintainer/release steward | Ownership map, CI gates, change control, incident/failure policy, artifact retention, and release checklist. |

Turn existing scripts and slides into a maintained communication pipeline: every named architecture deck has a source outline, speaker script, evidence/version stamp, and generated notes. The dashboard’s 90-second tour should point to the appropriate documentation tier and record only browser-local completion until a privacy-reviewed analytics policy exists.

**Acceptance gate:** a newcomer can complete the symmetry example and correctly distinguish `holds`, `fail`, and `unverifiable`; an adapter author can produce a candidate package without reading kernel internals; an auditor can independently replay a published bundle.

### Wave E6 — Governance, research, and archival controls

Operationalize change control through ADRs and registry policies. Every proposed adapter, empirical method binding, evidence schema, public claim, and documentation promotion must have an owner, version, test/evidence target, non-claims, and approval state. Add an archive intake workflow that preserves theory-specific research provenance while forbidding it from becoming a runtime evaluator branch without admission.

Add a claim/limitation register to the documentation site. It will distinguish **tested contract**, **mathematical definition**, **formal reference**, **empirical packet**, **external analysis receipt**, **research hypothesis**, and **interpretation**. The display wording will be generated from the class, not hand-authored per page.

**Acceptance gate:** every externally visible claim has a claim class, source/evidence link, scope, owner, and limitation; the release steward can block publication when any required field is missing.

