## Testing and release policy

Every new schema, adapter, packet, script, and document manifest will have success, invalid-input, unsupported-version, replay, and one-field tamper fixtures. Tests will run in the isolated core boundary without importing legacy experimental modules. Cross-language parity remains limited to the existing shared fixture boundary until Rust coverage is extended for the new adapter/packet representations; no claim of cross-language support may be made beforehand.

Documentation is also a release artifact: broken canonical links, missing status labels, unclassified theory-specific material, generated example drift, or a mismatch between a displayed test status and a current matrix must fail the documentation/release gate.

## Explicit non-goals for this ecosystem build

This plan does not deploy a live peer-to-peer consensus network, operate public transparency infrastructure, manage production keys, ingest confidential datasets, execute statistical inference automatically, certify FAIR compliance, establish any theory of reality, or treat archival research as admitted kernel semantics. Those future programs require their own operational, legal, scientific, security, and governance decisions.

## Risks and controls

| Risk | Trigger | Control | Residual boundary |
|---|---|---|---|
| Documentation sprawl | Multiple contradictory guides remain active | Canonical manifest, status labels, archive banners, CI link validation | Historical sources remain accessible but clearly non-canonical. |
| Theory lock-in returns through adapters | Framework label affects dispatch or documentation wording | Neutrality tests, code-owned registry, provenance-only framework IDs | Semantics still need independent review. |
| Empirical overclaiming | Packet/analysis result presented as a proof | Distinct claim classes, external-analysis boundary, non-claim templates | Scientific validity depends on method and domain evidence. |
| Starter kit creates low-quality adapters | Candidate lacks meaningful fixtures/review | Eight gates and quarantine status | Admission review costs time and expertise. |
| Dashboard status drifts from artifacts | Curated UI constants replace evidence feed | Generate dashboard feed from manifests; tests against artifact fixtures | UI remains an evidence viewer, not a source of truth. |
| Offline assurance is mistaken for production trust | Local Merkle/DSSE results are marketed as service guarantees | “local-only” labels, release boundary pages, blocked operational roadmap | Production trust services remain separate work. |

## Completion criteria

The ecosystem build is complete when the documentation manifest is canonical; each public adapter has synchronized generated examples; an external candidate can be admitted or quarantined by the eight-gate validator; empirical packets and analysis receipts are versioned and fail closed; release evidence produces the dashboard data; all documentation tiers are navigable; and the full suite has current, reproducible verification artifacts. At that point, additional research adapters and empirical methods become governed content additions rather than new architecture work.
